==Latvian==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|bat-pro|lv}} {{m|bat-pro|*aw-}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|lv}} {{m|ine-pro|*ew-}}, {{m|ine-pro|*ow-}} “to tie, to bind” > “to put on, to dress”. The original meaning was probably “to tie, to wrap (around the foot)”, whence “to put on (footwear)”. Cognates include {{etyl|lt|-}} {{term|auti|aũti|lang=lt}}, {{etyl|cu|-}} {{term|обути|lang=cu}} (< {{m|sla-pro|*uti}}), {{etyl|ru|-}} {{term|обуть|tr=obút’|lang=ru}}, {{etyl|be|-}} {{term|абуць|tr=abúc’|lang=be}}, {{etyl|uk|-}} {{term|обути|tr=obúty|lang=ru}}, {{etyl|bg|-}} {{term|обуя|tr=obúja|lang=bg}}, {{etyl|cs|-}} {{term|obouti|lang=cs}}, {{etyl|pl|-}} {{term|obuć|lang=pl}}, {{etyl|la|-}} {{term|exuere||to take off|lang=la}} (< {{m|la|*ex-u-ere}}).<ref>{{R:lv:LEV}}</ref>

===Pronunciation===
* {{lv-IPA|àwt|falling}}
{{audio|lv-riga-aut.ogg|lang=lv}}

===Verb===
{{lv-verb|tr|1st|aun|aun||āv|auj|auj}}

# [[put on]] [[footwear]] ([[shoe]]s, [[boot]]s, [[sock]]s, etc.)
#: ''zēns '''āva''' kājas'' — the boy '''put on''' footwear (lit. on his feet)
#: '''''aut''' kājas pastalās'' — to '''put on''' pastalas (simple footwear) (lit. to put one's feet into pastalas)
#: '''''aut''' kurpes kājas'' — to '''put on''' shoes (lit. to put shoes on one's feet)
# ''nosēdos uz akmens un gribēju '''aut''' kājas, bet kurpes bija ļoti sabristas'' — I sat down on a rock and wanted to '''put shoes on''' (lit. to '''put''' (my) feet (into shoes)), but the shoes were very wet
#: ''Žanis '''āva''' kājās stulmeņu zābakus'' — Žanis '''put''' the long boots '''on''' (his) feet
# {{context|figuratively|with {{l/lv|kājas}}|lang=lv}} to [[prepare]] for a [[journey]] (lit. to '''put on footwear''')
#: ''un tūliņ ķēniņš '''aun kājas''' savu sievu meklēt'' — and quickly the king '''puts on footwear''' to go looking for his wife

====Conjugation====
{{lv-conj|au|aun|1st|aun||āv||n/v-l|stem2=auj|width=460}}

====Usage notes====
Note that {{l/lv|aut}} can take two complements, the footwear or the subject's feet. Either can be the direct object, in which case the other will be a locative complement (i.e., either "to put shoes on one's feet" or "to put one's feet into shoes").

